    a: "<b>Anticholinergic toxidrome:</b><br><br><b>Peripheral anticholinergic effects (M1–M3 blockade):</b><ul><li><b>'Mad as a hatter'</b> — confusion, delirium</li><li><b>'Blind as a bat'</b> — blurred vision (mydriasis, loss of accommodation)</li><li><b>'Dry as a bone'</b> — anhidrosis, dry mouth, dry skin</li><li><b>'Red as a beet'</b> — flushing</li><li><b>'Hot as Hades'</b> — hyperthermia (no sweating)</li><li><b>'Full as a flask'</b> — urinary retention</li><li>Tachycardia, constipation, ileus</li></ul><b>Central effects:</b> Agitation, confusion, delirium, hallucinations, seizures<br><br><b>Elderly vs. young:</b><ul><li>Elderly: <b>disproportionately affected at lower doses</b> due to age-related decline in central cholinergic neurons (basal forebrain), reduced hepatic clearance, decreased renal excretion</li><li>Even low-dose anticholinergics cause significant cognitive impairment in elderly</li></ul>"
